## 11. MEMORY DATABASE AND PERSISTENCE

Inspect the current application and choose a persistence solution appropriate to its architecture.

If the application already has a database, reuse it.

If no persistent backend exists, use a suitable persistent database such as Firebase Firestore or another properly supported database.

Do not rely on in-memory JavaScript variables as permanent storage.

Do not use browser localStorage as the sole source of truth for a multi-device, account-based long-term memory system.

A local-only prototype may use local storage as a clearly identified temporary fallback, but it must not be presented as production-grade cloud persistence.

Create a structured memory schema similar to:

Memory {
  id: string
  userId: string
  title: string
  content: string
  category: Identity | Preferences | Life | Active |
            Relationships | Milestones | Behaviors
  tags: string[]
  source: Manual | Automatic
  status: Active | Planned | Completed | Archived
  verificationStatus: UserVerified | AIExtracted | Uncertain
  confidence: number | null
  createdAt: timestamp
  updatedAt: timestamp
  lastRecalledAt: timestamp | null
  recallCount: number
  relatedMemoryIds: string[]
  sourceConversationId: string | null
}

Adapt this schema to the actual database's requirements. Do not store fabricated metadata or meaningless default confidence scores.

Implement:

- Unique memory IDs
- Proper user ownership
- Database reads and writes
- Input validation
- Safe updates and deletes
- Loading and error handling
- Database indexes where required
- Pagination for larger memory collections
- Concurrency handling where appropriate
- Appropriate timestamps
- Database access rules and authorization

For multi-user applications, every read, update, and deletion must enforce ownership on the server side.

Never expose privileged database credentials or server-side secrets in client-side code.

If authentication already exists, integrate with it. If authentication does not exist, do not pretend that user-specific cloud memory is securely isolated without implementing an appropriate identity and authorization mechanism.

